# Endings: checked in this order on arrival day; the last one has no conditions.
# Each ending is one carrier clause (the fleet's hail vote) times one arrival clause
# (the storm vote: held the plan, waited it out in orbit, or no storm). Every ending
# closes on the first crate. `title` is the heading; see STORY_BIBLE.md.

card end_lights
type ending
cat ending
title Lights On
when flag signal_answered
when flag arrive_hold
text The fleet holds the plan it voted for and goes straight down into the storm. At four kilometres the cameras see only brown; the radar has the ground but can't say which ground is flat. Then Cargo 2, answered in time at last, turns on every light it has and leaves them on. Seven ships land beside it, a careful kilometre off. In the hold, the first crate has Sharpie on it: "You'll thank me."

card end_through
type ending
cat ending
title Through the Dust
when flag arrive_hold
text The fleet holds the plan it voted for and goes straight down into the storm, cameras blind, radar sure of the ground and nothing else. Out of the brown, every eleven seconds, a light, still asking. Seven ships land beside it, a careful kilometre off, and the first suit out keys the old channel from close enough. The light stops blinking. In the hold, the first crate has Sharpie on it: "You'll thank me."

card end_long_orbit
type ending
cat ending
title The Long Orbit
when flag signal_answered
when flag arrive_wait
text Aerocapture, as voted, then four nights of ration math in a long orbit, waiting out the dust. Answered at last, Cargo 2 sends a little more of its manifest every pass, in a format nobody has flown in years, and the fleet reads it aloud like letters from home. When the sky clears, seven ships go down to collect. In the hold, the first crate has Sharpie on it: "You'll thank me."

card end_patience
type ending
cat ending
title Patience
when flag arrive_wait
text Aerocapture, as voted, then four nights of half portions in a long orbit. Every night, through the dust, one point of light keeps time beside the landing zone, still asking. When the sky clears, seven ships land a careful kilometre from it, and the first suit out keys the old channel from close enough. The light holds steady. In the hold, the first crate has Sharpie on it: "You'll thank me."

card end_friend
type ending
cat ending
title Old Friend
when flag signal_answered
text Seven ships come down in clear air, a careful kilometre from a ship that waited four years for someone close enough to answer in time. Its lights hold steady now. On the walk over, Cargo 2 sends its whole manifest, slowly, like something that has been rehearsing. In the hold, the first crate has Sharpie on it, in a hand they know: "You'll thank me." Beside it, a little ship with its fingers crossed.

card end_close
type ending
cat ending
title Close Enough
text Seven ships land in clear air. Afterwards they drive Cargo 4's rover out to Cargo 2, past its long shadow, and key the old channel from two hundred metres: the first answer in four years to arrive while it's listening. The light stops blinking and stays on. In the hold, the first crate has Sharpie on it, in a hand they know: "You'll thank me." They say it aloud, one at a time.
